---

<Base
  title="ST Bus Tracking in Gujarat — Live GSRTC Bus Tracker"
  description="Compare every way Gujarat commuters find their ST bus — calling the depot, asking at the stand, official app — and how live ST bus tracking saves time."
  path="st-bus-tracking"
  lang="en"
  ogType="article"
  headerVariant="page"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <main id="main">

<section class="wrap section narrow">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>ST bus tracking in Gujarat, explained</h1>
  <p class="lede">Most Gujarat commuters still find their ST bus the old way — a phone call, a guess, or standing at the stand and waiting to see. Here's what each of those actually gets you, and where live tracking saves real time.</p>

  <form class="plate" id="plate-form" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
    <label class="sr" for="plate">Bus number plate</label>
    <input id="plate" name="plate" type="text" inputmode="latin" autocomplete="off" placeholder="GJ-18-ZT-1028">
    <button class="btn primary" type="submit">Track</button>
  </form>
</section>

<section class="wrap section narrow">
  <h2 class="reveal">How people track an ST bus today</h2>

  <h3 class="reveal">Calling the depot</h3>
  <p class="reveal">Reliable when someone picks up, but the person answering usually has no better view of the bus than you do — they're reading the same schedule, not a live feed. It also only works during depot hours.</p>

  <h3 class="reveal">Asking at the stand</h3>
  <p class="reveal">"It usually comes around this time" is a habit, not a position. It works fine on a normal day and tells you nothing on a day the bus is running late, replaced, or cancelled.</p>

  <h3 class="reveal">The printed or app timetable</h3>
  <p class="reveal">A schedule is a plan, not a report. It's the right starting point for which buses exist on a route, but it can't tell you that today's 6:40 is running 20 minutes behind — only where it's supposed to be.</p>

  <h3 class="reveal">Live GPS tracking</h3>
  <p class="reveal">This is the one method reading the bus's actual position. It answers the specific question a timetable can't: where is this bus <em>right now</em>, and does that mean I should leave for the stand yet. See <a href="/gsrtc-live-bus-tracking">how the live position itself works</a>.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">Two ways to search</h2>
  <p class="reveal">If you already know the bus — you saw it leave, or a driver told you the plate — search by number and go straight to its live map. If you don't, search by the two stations you're travelling between instead: ST Tracker lists every scheduled and running service on that pair, with live countdowns for the ones already on the road.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Both paths lead to the same live map, the same stop-by-stop route, and the same option to set an alert for the stop you're waiting at.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">What "tracking" adds beyond a timetable</h2>
  <ul class="reveal">
    <li>The bus's current position and speed, not its scheduled one.</li>
    <li>How far it is from your stop, updated as it moves.</li>
    <li>Whether it's actually running late, and by how much — not a guess.</li>
    <li>An alert on your phone shortly before it arrives, so you don't have to keep checking.</li>
    <li>Whether other riders report it crowded, replaced, or a no-show, before you commit to waiting for it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="reveal">Want the specific steps to find one bus's position right now? See <a href="/st-bus-live-location">finding your ST bus's live location, step by step</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap ad-slot-wrap" aria-label="Advertisement">
  <p class="ad-label">Advertisement</p>
  <ins class="adsbygoogle ad-slot" style="display:block" data-ad-client="ca-pub-7164999486301748" data-ad-slot="8460038937" data-ad-format="auto" data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
</section>

<section id="faq" class="band">
  <div class="wrap section narrow">
    <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details class="reveal" name="faq"><summary>What is an ST bus?</summary>
        <p>ST stands for State Transport — the buses run by GSRTC (Gujarat State Road Transport Corporation) across the state. "ST bus" and "GSRTC bus" refer to the same fleet; ST is simply the older, more common name among riders.</p></details>
      <details class="reveal" name="faq"><summary>Can I track an ST bus without knowing its number?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Search by the two stations you're travelling between instead of a plate number, and pick any departure from the list of live and upcoming buses.</p></details>
      <details class="reveal" name="faq"><summary>Does ST bus tracking work for private buses too?</summary>
        <p>No. GSRTC Bus Tracker only covers GSRTC's own ST fleet, since that is the operator whose position data it reads. Private travel operators run separate systems.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="wrap section closer">
  <h2>Stop guessing where the bus is</h2>
  <p class="sub">Type a plate, or search a route. Free, no account.</p>
  <a class="btn primary lg" href="https://tracker.shivrajsinh.in">Open GSRTC Bus Tracker</a>
</section>

</main>
</Base>
